if [ "${compile_rc}" -ne 0 ]; then
  print_result "FAIL"
  exit "${compile_rc}"
fi

"${TB_DIR}/simv" -l "${RUN_LOG}"
run_rc=$?

if [ "${run_rc}" -eq 0 ] && grep -q "Observed irq_id=3 as expected" "${RUN_LOG}"; then
  print_result "PASS"
else
  print_result "FAIL"
  if [ "${run_rc}" -eq 0 ]; then
    run_rc=1
  fi
fi

exit "${run_rc}"

resolved_issues.body:
Hi Michael, 

I have a very corner case on clearing accumulation count using `class_clr` registers. The issue is: `class_ctrl` reg cleared `accum_cnt`, but escalation still triggered. Please refer to the screenshot as an example:
In my screenshot:
1). `classc_accum_thresh` = 1
2). `classc_accum_cnt` is transition from 0 to 1
3). `classc_clr` is set when  `classc_accum_cnt` is transition from 0 to 1
From the screenshot, even though next cycle `classc_accum_cnt` is cleared by `classc_clr`, but class_c still goes to escalation states.

I think a quick solution could be adding one more gating to the state machine in `alert_handler_esc_timer.sv`:
```
      Idle: begin
        cnt_clr = 1'b1;

        if (accum_trig_i && en_i) begin  -> change to: if (accum_trig_i && en_i  && !clr_i) begin
          state_d    = Phase0;
          cnt_en     = 1'b1;
          esc_trig_o = 1'b1;
        // the counter is zero in this state. so if the
        // timeout count is zero (==disabled), cnt_ge will be true.
        end else if (timeout_en_i && !cnt_ge && en_i) begin
          cnt_en  = 1'b1;
          state_d = Timeout;
        end
      end
```

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/11466553/82475955-28d9a680-9a82-11ea-8b2c-da23a16f950f.png)

